    - When PC0 sends a packet to 192.168.20.20, it first reaches R1
    - How does R1 know that 192.168.20.20 is behind R2:
    - Does R1 automatically know or do we have to tell R1 ??

    Topology:
    ---------
    PC0 ---- SW1 ---- R1 ===== R2 ---- SW2 ---- PC1
    192.168.10.0      10.10.10.0      192.168.20.0

    - R1 knows only the networks directly connected to it:
      192.168.10.0/24
      10.10.10.0/30

    - But dose R1 know this network (192.168.20.0/24), NO.
    - So if PC0 sends a packet to 192.168.20.20, R1 receives it and thinks:
    - I know 192.168.10.0, I know 10.10.10.0
    - But what is 192.168.20.0, I have never heard of it
    - So the router drops the packet

    This is why Static Routing exits:
    ---------------------------------
    We manually tell R1:
        - If you never want to reach 192.168.20.0/24
        - Send the packet to 10.10.10.2

    Similarly, we tell R2:
        - If you never want to reach 192.168.10.0/24
        - Send packet to 10.10.10.2

    * That is exactly what the ip route command does


Q) Notice the link between R1 and R2: Why did we choose /30 instead of /24 (255.255.255.0)
    R1 -------- R2
    10.10.10.0/30

    - How many device are connected on that link:
    - How many usable IP address do we actually need:

    Look at the Topology:
    PC0 -------- R1 ========== R2 -------- PC1
                (This link)

    Focus only on this cable:
    R1 ------------ R2
        - How many devices are connected to this cable:
          (Only R1, R2)
          So we need only 2 IP address

    Then why not use /24:
    ---------------------
    Let's see what /24 gives us.
    Subnet: 10.10.10.0/24
    (Number of IP address, Total address = 256, usable IP address=254)
    But we only have: R1, R2 = Only 2 devices
    If we use /24, 254 usable IPs, Only 2 are used and 252 are wasted

    Now let's use /30:
    ------------------
    10.10.10.0/30
    - How many IPs: 10.10.10.0 Network Address
      10.10.10.1 R1
      10.10.10.2 R2
      10.10.10.3 Broadcast
      Only: 2 usable IP address
      So when we used /30 nothing is wasted, only 2 used

    Comparison:
    -----------
    /24:
        - Total IPs (256), Usable IPs (254), Suitable for LAN with many devices

    /30:
        - Total IPs (4), Usable IPs (2), Suitable for Point-To-Point link between two routers


Q) When PC0 wants to send a packet to PC1, who makes the first routing decision: PC0
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    PC0
    192.168.10.10
           |
           |
    R1
    192.168.10.1
    10.10.10.1
           |
           |
    R2
    10.10.10.2
    192.168.20.1
           |
           |
    PC1
    192.168.20.20


    Packet Flow:
    ------------
    Suppose:
        PC0
        IP: 192.168.10.0/24
        Gateway: 192.168.10.1
        Destination: 192.168.20.20

        - The first think PC0 does is ask: is 192.168.20.20 is my own network?
          PC0 compares: My network 192.168.10.0/24, Destination 192.168.20.20
          It realizes: 192.168.20.20 # 192.168.10.0/24
          So PC0 decides: This destination is outside my network, I must send it to my default gateway (192.168.10.1)
          -> This is first routing decision
          -> The router has not even seen the packet yet, The PC itself decided: I cannot send directly to the destination, I will send it to my gateway

        Then what happens:
            PC0
               │
               │ Ethernet Frame
               ▼
            Switch
               │
               ▼
            Router R1

            - the router does not make any routing decision
            - it simply looks at the destination MAC address and forwards the frame

        Router R1 receives the packet:
            - now R1 removes the Ethernet header looks at the destination IP: 192.168.20.20
            - it checks its routing table: if it has a route: 192.168.20.0/24 --> Next Hop 10.10.10.2
            - Then it forwards the packet to R2

        Router R2 receives the packet:
            - It checks: 192.168.20.0/24, It says: This network is directly connected me
            - So it sends the packet to PC1

Complete Flow:
--------------
    Step 1
    PC0 checks destination IP.

    ↓

    Step 2
    PC0 decides:
    Different network.
    Send to default gateway.

    ↓

    Step 3
    Switch forwards the Ethernet frame.

    ↓

    Step 4
    Router R1 checks its routing table.

    ↓

    Step 5
    R1 forwards to R2.

    ↓

    Step 6
    R2 sees the destination network is directly connected.

    ↓

    Step 7
    R2 sends the packet to PC1.

Q) Which devices are Layer 2 devices, Which devices are Layer 3 devices:
------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Switches are Layer 2 devices: WHY:
        - Because switches make forwarding decisions based on MAC address

    Routers are Layer 3 devices: WHY:
        - Because routers make forwarding decisions based on IP address


Q) Which devices are responsible for: (Forwarding frames using MAC addresses, Forwarding packets using IP addresses)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    - Switches forwards frames using MAC addresses
    - Routers forward packets using IP address

Q) PC0 wants to send data to PC1:
------------------------------
    PC0 ----- SW1 ----- R1 ===== R2 ----- SW2 ----- PC1
    > Now let's see what each device is actually doing:

    1) PC0 Creates a Packet:
        Source IP: 192.168.10.10
        Destination: 192.168.20.20

        Then it creates an Ethernet Frame:
            Source MAC,
            Destination MAC (Default Gateway)

        - The IP address is the final destination
        - The MAC address is only for the next device

    2) Switch Receives the Frame: The switch never looks at this:
        (Destination IP 192.168.20.20, It doesn't care)
        > Instead it checks:
          Destination MAC

        - Then it asks: Which port has this MAC address
        - If it knows: it forwards the frame
        - If it doesn't knw: It flood the frame

    3) Router Receives the Packet:
        - Now Router1 R1 removes the Ether header:
        - Now it checks: Destination IP: 192.168.20.20
        - The router never checks MAC address to decide where the packet should go next,
          It uses the routing table and the destination IP network
        - Then it says: I know how to reach this network
        - OR, I dont' know this network
        # That is routing

Q) Does a switch check the destination IP address before forwarding a frame: NO
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    - A Layer 2 switch forwards Ethernet Frame based on the destination MAC address,
    - It does not use the destination IP address for forwarding decisions

Q) Does a router check the destination MAC address before routing the packet:
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    - The router removes the incoming Layer 2 header
    - Examines the destination IP address, consults its routing table,
    - And then creates a new layer 2 frame with new source and destination MAC address for the next hop

Q) When PC0 sends the first Ethernet frame:
-------------------------------------------
    PC0
    IP  = 192.168.10.10
    MAC = AAAA

    ↓

    Switch

    ↓

    Router R1
    IP  = 192.168.10.1
    MAC = BBBB

    - Now PC0 wants to send data to PC1 (192.168.20.20).
    - what is the Destination MAC Address:

    1) PC0 wants to send a packet:
        PC0
        IP : 192.168.10.10
        MAC: AAAA

        ↓

        Destination IP
        192.168.20.20

        - PC0 first checks: is 192.268.20.20 my subnet:
        - It calculates: My Network 192.168.10.0.24
        - Destination: 192.168.20.20
        - No, Different Network
        - So PC0 decides: I will send this packet to my Default Gateway
        - Who is the Default gateway: 192.168.10.1 (Router R1)

    2) But PC0 doesn't know Router R1's MAC address:
        - Now PC0 asks: Who has IP 192.168.10.1
        - This is where ARP comes in.
        - PC0 sends an ARP Request: Destination MAC of the ARP Request (FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF)
        - This is the Broadcast MAC address

    3) Router Replies:
        - 192.168.10.1 is 00:11:22:33:44:55
        - This is an ARP Replay
        - Now PC0 stores: 192.168.10.1 -> 00:11:22:33:44:55 in its ARP cache

    4) Now PC0 sends the Actual Data:
        - Now that PC0 knows the router's MAC address, it creates the real Ethernet Frame

        The Frame Looks Like:
        ---------------------
            Ethernet Header

            Destination MAC

            Router R1 MAC
            (00:11:22:33:44:55)

            Source MAC

            PC0 MAC

            ----------------------

            IP Packet

            Source IP

            192.168.10.10

            Destination IP

            192.168.20.20

The IP Header: Never changes
----------------------------
    Source IP
    192.168.10.10
    Destination IP
    192.168.20.20

The Ethernet Header:
--------------------
    First hop: PC0 - Router R1 MAC
    Second hop: Router R1 - Router R2 MAC
    Third hop: Router R2 - PC1 MAC

Q) When PC0 sends the first Ethernet frame, what is the destination MAC:
------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Router R1's MAC Address: The broadcast MAC (FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF)
    is used only for the ARP Request to discover the router's MAC address

Q) Does the switch know the destination IP address: NO
------------------------------------------------------
    - A Layer 2 switch forwards Ethernet frame based on destination MAC address,
    - If the sender does not know the MAC address of the default gateway,
    - It first sends an ARP Request using the broadcast MAC address (FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF)
    - After receiving the ARP replay, the actual data frame is sent with the router's MAC address
      as the destination MAC.































